<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Electronics on 0x32++</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/electronics/</link><description>Recent content in Electronics on 0x32++</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0100</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/electronics/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Next Project: ESP32-S3 and a 4.2 Inch E-Ink Display</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/esp32-s3-eink-display-next-project/</link><pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/esp32-s3-eink-display-next-project/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;After &lt;a href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/esp32-wifi-scanner-case/"&gt;designing a case for my ESP32 WiFi scanner&lt;/a&gt; I&amp;rsquo;ve picked up some new parts to play with: a &lt;a href="https://github.com/WeActStudio/WeActStudio.ESP32S3CoreBoard"&gt;WeAct Studio ESP32-S3-N16R8 dev board&lt;/a&gt; — 16MB of flash and 8MB of PSRAM, dual USB-C, RGB status LED, sat in a little printed case I already had lying around — paired with a &lt;a href="https://github.com/WeActStudio/WeActStudio.EpaperModule"&gt;WeAct Studio 4.2 inch e-paper module&lt;/a&gt;, 400x300 resolution over a simple SPI header (GND, SDA, SCL, CS, D/C, RES, BUSY, VCC).&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Designing and Printing a Case for My ESP32 WiFi Scanner</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/esp32-wifi-scanner-case/</link><pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/esp32-wifi-scanner-case/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;After &lt;a href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/tinkercad-unifi-router-shelf/"&gt;designing my first shelf in TinkerCAD&lt;/a&gt;, I wanted a project that actually needed a proper enclosure rather than a mounting bracket. I&amp;rsquo;d been building a small self-contained WiFi scanner around &lt;a href="https://www.aliexpress.com/item/1005005970553639.html"&gt;this cheap ESP32 dev board&lt;/a&gt; — an ESP32 module with a built-in 1.14 inch LCD — paired with a small rechargeable battery, and it had been living on my desk as a loose tangle of board, wires and cells. Time to give it a case.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>TFT Display ST7735 MicroPython</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico8/</link><pubDate>Fri, 17 Feb 2023 16:31:44 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico8/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I got a cheap SPI TFT ST7735 Display module with SD Card reader off ebay, it had an unusual number of pins on the back.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="wp-caption alignnone"&gt;&#10; &lt;a data-flickr-embed="true" data-header="true" data-footer="false"&#10; data-context="false"&#10; href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/52700051851"&#10; title="Numbers"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52700051851_da6a2aab59_c.jpg" width="800" alt="Numbers" /&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;script async src="//embedr.flickr.com/assets/client-code.js" charset="utf-8"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption class="wp-caption-text"&gt;Numbers&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;p&gt;It took a while searching about on the internet and some head scratching to find a library and work out what connected to what on the Pico.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;This is what I found, and worked out.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;table&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;thead&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;th&gt;TFT Board&lt;/th&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;th&gt;Raspberry Pi Pin&lt;/th&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;/thead&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;tbody&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;LED&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;3v3(Out)&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;SCK&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GP10&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;SDA&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GP11&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;AO/DC&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GP16&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;Reset&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GP17&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;CS&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GP18&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GND&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;GND&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;VCC&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;VBUS 5V&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;/tbody&gt;&#10;&lt;/table&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Using that information I was able to put together this library based on a few others I found and a few test programs.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Small LED Matrix Numbers</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico7/</link><pubDate>Thu, 26 Jan 2023 15:16:16 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico7/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Something I did a few (8) years ago on the Arduino to display two-digit numbers on a 8x8 LED matrix, this is an update to do the same in Micropthon.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Numbers have been defined to 3x5 pixels, so for a two-digit number it fits into 8x5 LED grid with spacing, saving some LED Matrix space.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="wp-caption alignnone"&gt;&#10; &lt;a data-flickr-embed="true" data-header="true" data-footer="false"&#10; data-context="false"&#10; href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/52650530060"&#10; title="Numbers"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52650530060_6ca8331919_w.jpg" width="800" alt="Numbers" /&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;script async src="//embedr.flickr.com/assets/client-code.js" charset="utf-8"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption class="wp-caption-text"&gt;Numbers&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The code for this can be found here &lt;a href="https://github.com/alastairhm/pico_w/blob/main/numbers.py"&gt;numbers.py&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>DHT11 Sensor in Micropython</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico6/</link><pubDate>Mon, 23 Jan 2023 16:39:29 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico6/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Just a short bit of code to test a DHT11 temperature and humidity sensor module.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;This version has just three pins VCC, GND and data, so easy to connect and use with the DHT library.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/alastairhm/pico_w/blob/main/dht11.py"&gt;dht11.py&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="wp-caption alignnone"&gt;&#10; &lt;a data-flickr-embed="true" data-header="true" data-footer="false"&#10; data-context="false"&#10; href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/52644001692"&#10; title="DHT11 Module"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/52644001692_37acf2e91a_c.jpg" width="800" alt="DHT11 Module" /&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;script async src="//embedr.flickr.com/assets/client-code.js" charset="utf-8"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption class="wp-caption-text"&gt;DHT11 Module&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;</description></item><item><title>Raspberry Pi Pico W LED Matrix</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico5/</link><pubDate>Fri, 20 Jan 2023 09:56:40 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico5/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Found a LED Matrix with a Max7219 in my parts bin (box of random electronics in the garage) so looked into getting it working with the Pico and MicroPython.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;This is an example why I love open source.&#10;The original library I found was functional but sparse, last committed to 4 years ago.&#10;There were a few forks, most not much activity.&#10;One had been active recently and had some major improvements in functionality so switched over to that fork.&#10;&lt;a href="https://github.com/enchant97/micropython-max7219"&gt;https://github.com/enchant97/micropython-max7219&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Raspberry Pi Pico W Mandelbrot Set</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico4/</link><pubDate>Tue, 17 Jan 2023 12:29:25 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/pico4/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Stumbled across a Python script to run a Mandelbrot set in ASCII in Python so I thought I would see how it ran on the RaspberryPi Pico.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;After tweaking it slightly from the &lt;a href="http://warp.povusers.org/MandScripts/python.html"&gt;source&lt;/a&gt; it ended up like this &lt;a href="https://github.com/alastairhm/pico_w/blob/main/mandelbrot.py"&gt;mandelbrot.py&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;This results in the following;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="code-block"&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="code-copy" type="button" hidden aria-label="Copy code to clipboard"&gt;&#10; &lt;span class="code-copy-label" aria-hidden="true"&gt;Copy&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/button&gt;&#10; &lt;pre tabindex="0"&gt;&lt;code&gt; ...................................................................................................&#10; .....................................................................................................&#10; .......................................................................................................&#10; .........................................................................................................&#10; ...................................,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,..........................................